<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
    <dict>
        <key>Description</key>
        <string>Downloads the current release of Stellarium for Windows (the public Qt6 x64 .exe installer asset of the newest non-pre-release on GitHub that has a matching file, kept under the asset's own file name), checks it against the SHA-256 that GitHub records for the asset, and takes the version label from the asset's file name: the one group of SEARCH_PATTERN, checked for its shape (two to four numbers joined by dots). The recipe takes the Qt6 build for 64-bit Windows; the same release also holds a Qt5 build, a Qt6 build for ARM64 and, in older releases, 32-bit builds, which the pattern does not take. Whether the installer has a version resource of its own was NOT read (nothing was downloaded), so the label is taken from the file's name, and the release's tag is not the source because the tag of one release differs from the version in its file's name. Whether the installed program reports the same version is NOT established: the first dry run is read as the README says. The asset names are those of GitHub's release list of 2026-11-13. AutoPkg takes the first release that has a matching file however old it is, so a new spelling would send the recipe back to an older release: read the line that names the selected asset and release in the first dry run. The recipe never stops on an unchanged download, so a failed import is tried again at the next run. Pre-releases are skipped unless the override sets the input include_prereleases to true. The step GitHubNewestReleaseGuard, right after the provider, stops the run when the release that the provider selected is not the newest release (a pre-release is skipped unless include_prereleases is true): it names the newest release and its files. The override's input accepted_newest_release, set to the tag of a newest release that has no Windows installer file (.msi, .exe, .msix) on purpose, lets the run go on only while that release is the newest, and release_tag_pattern names the line of releases to follow for a vendor with two lines (every run names the newer releases that it left out).</string>
        <key>Identifier</key>
        <string>com.github.serrc-techops.download.Stellarium-Win</string>
        <key>Input</key>
        <dict>
            <key>NAME</key>
            <string>Stellarium</string>
            <key>SEARCH_PATTERN</key>
            <string>stellarium-([0-9]+(?:\.[0-9]+)+)-qt6-win64\.exe$</string>
            <key>accepted_newest_release</key>
            <string></string>
            <key>include_prereleases</key>
            <false/>
            <key>release_tag_pattern</key>
            <string></string>
        </dict>
        <key>MinimumVersion</key>
        <string>1.0.0</string>
        <key>Process</key>
        <array>
            <dict>
                <key>Processor</key>
                <string>GitHubReleasesInfoProvider</string>
                <key>Arguments</key>
                <dict>
                    <key>github_repo</key>
                    <string>Stellarium/stellarium</string>
                    <key>asset_regex</key>
                    <string>%SEARCH_PATTERN%</string>
                </dict>
            </dict>
            <dict>
                <key>Processor</key>
                <string>com.github.serrc-techops.SharedProcessors/GitHubNewestReleaseGuard</string>
                <key>Arguments</key>
                <dict>
                    <key>github_asset_api_url</key>
                    <string>%asset_url%</string>
                </dict>
            </dict>
            <dict>
                <key>Processor</key>
                <string>URLDownloader</string>
            </dict>
            <dict>
                <key>Processor</key>
                <string>EndOfCheckPhase</string>
            </dict>
            <dict>
                <key>Processor</key>
                <string>com.github.serrc-techops.SharedProcessors/GitHubAssetDigest</string>
                <key>Arguments</key>
                <dict>
                    <key>github_asset_api_url</key>
                    <string>%asset_url%</string>
                </dict>
            </dict>
            <dict>
                <key>Processor</key>
                <string>com.github.serrc-techops.SharedProcessors/ChecksumVerifier</string>
                <key>Arguments</key>
                <dict>
                    <key>pathname</key>
                    <string>%pathname%</string>
                    <key>expected_sha256</key>
                    <string>%expected_sha256%</string>
                </dict>
            </dict>
            <dict>
                <key>Processor</key>
                <string>com.github.serrc-techops.SharedProcessors/VersionFromName</string>
                <key>Arguments</key>
                <dict>
                    <key>pathname</key>
                    <string>%pathname%</string>
                    <key>version_pattern</key>
                    <string>%SEARCH_PATTERN%</string>
                </dict>
            </dict>
        </array>
    </dict>
</plist>
